## Part (a)

The temperature $u(r,t)$ in a circular plate with insulated faces satisfies the radially symmetric heat equation

$$\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} \;=\; k\!\left(\frac{\partial^{2}u}{\partial r^{2}}+\frac{1}{r}\frac{\partial u}{\partial r}\right),\qquad 0\le r\le c,$$

with boundary condition $u(c,t)=0$ (edge held at zero), regularity at $r=0$, and initial condition

$$u(r,0)=f(r)=\begin{cases}\dfrac{q_{0}}{\pi s\,\epsilon^{2}},&0\le r\le\epsilon,\\[4pt]0,&\epsilon<r\le c.\end{cases}$$

(The total heat is $\int_{0}^{c}\!s\,f(r)\,2\pi r\,dr=s\cdot\frac{q_{0}}{\pi s\epsilon^{2}}\cdot\pi\epsilon^{2}=q_{0}$, confirming $q_{0}$ calories are injected.)

**Separation of variables.** Set $u=R(r)T(t)$. Then $T'/kT = (R''+\frac{1}{r}R')/R = -\alpha^{2}$, giving
- $T(t)=e^{-\alpha^{2}kt}$ (decaying),
- $R''+\frac{1}{r}R'+\alpha^{2}R=0$, the Bessel equation of order $0$.

Regularity at $r=0$ rejects $Y_{0}$, so $R(r)=J_{0}(\alpha r)$. The boundary condition $R(c)=0$ requires
$$J_{0}(\alpha c)=0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \alpha_{n}=\frac{y_{n}}{c},\qquad n=1,2,\dots$$
where $\{y_{n}\}$ are the positive zeros of $J_{0}$.

Hence
$$u(r,t)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}A_{n}\,J_{0}\!\left(\frac{y_{n}r}{c}\right)\exp\!\left(-\frac{y_{n}^{2}kt}{c^{2}}\right). \tag{1}$$

**Determining the coefficients.** The eigenfunctions are orthogonal with weight $r$ on $[0,c]$:
$$\int_{0}^{c}r\,J_{0}\!\left(\frac{y_{n}r}{c}\right)J_{0}\!\left(\frac{y_{m}r}{c}\right)dr=\frac{c^{2}}{2}\,J_{1}(y_{n})^{2}\,\delta_{nm},$$
using $J_{0}'=-J_{1}$ and $J_{0}(y_{n})=0$. Setting $t=0$ in (1) and projecting against $rJ_{0}(y_{n}r/c)$:
$$A_{n}=\frac{2}{c^{2}J_{1}(y_{n})^{2}}\int_{0}^{c}r\,f(r)\,J_{0}\!\left(\frac{y_{n}r}{c}\right)dr.$$

Since $f$ vanishes for $r>\epsilon$,
$$A_{n}=\frac{2}{c^{2}J_{1}(y_{n})^{2}}\cdot\frac{q_{0}}{\pi s\,\epsilon^{2}}\int_{0}^{\epsilon}r\,J_{0}\!\left(\frac{y_{n}r}{c}\right)dr.$$

Using the standard identity $\displaystyle\int_{0}^{a}r\,J_{0}(br)\,dr=\frac{a}{b}J_{1}(ab)$ (from $\frac{d}{dr}[rJ_{1}(br)]=brJ_{0}(br)$), with $b=y_{n}/c$:
$$\int_{0}^{\epsilon}r\,J_{0}\!\left(\frac{y_{n}r}{c}\right)dr=\frac{\epsilon\,c}{y_{n}}\,J_{1}\!\left(\frac{y_{n}\epsilon}{c}\right).$$

Therefore
$$A_{n}=\frac{2q_{0}}{\pi s\,c\,\epsilon\,y_{n}\,J_{1}(y_{n})^{2}}\,J_{1}\!\left(\frac{y_{n}\epsilon}{c}\right),$$

and the solution is

$$\boxed{\,u(r,t)=\frac{2q_{0}}{\pi s\,c}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{J_{1}\!\left(\dfrac{y_{n}\epsilon}{c}\right)}{\epsilon\,y_{n}\,J_{1}(y_{n})^{2}}\exp\!\left(-\frac{y_{n}^{2}kt}{c^{2}}\right)J_{0}\!\left(\frac{y_{n}r}{c}\right).\,} \tag{2}$$

## Part (b)

Take $\epsilon\to 0$ in (2). Since $J_{1}(z)=\tfrac{z}{2}+O(z^{3})$ as $z\to 0$,
$$J_{1}\!\left(\frac{y_{n}\epsilon}{c}\right)=\frac{y_{n}\epsilon}{2c}+O(\epsilon^{3}),\qquad\text{so}\qquad \frac{J_{1}(y_{n}\epsilon/c)}{\epsilon\,y_{n}}\;\xrightarrow{\;\epsilon\to 0\;}\;\frac{1}{2c}.$$

Substituting into (2):
$$u(r,t)\;\longrightarrow\;\frac{2q_{0}}{\pi s\,c}\cdot\frac{1}{2c}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{J_{1}(y_{n})^{2}}\exp\!\left(-\frac{y_{n}^{2}kt}{c^{2}}\right)J_{0}\!\left(\frac{y_{n}r}{c}\right),$$

i.e.

$$\boxed{\,u(r,t)=\frac{q_{0}}{\pi s\,c^{2}}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{J_{1}(y_{n})^{2}}\exp\!\left(-\frac{y_{n}^{2}kt}{c^{2}}\right)J_{0}\!\left(\frac{y_{n}r}{c}\right),\,}$$

which is precisely the temperature distribution resulting from injecting $q_{0}$ calories at the center of the plate, where $\{y_{n}\}_{1}^{\infty}$ are the positive roots of $J_{0}(x)=0$.

